==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|un-|employment|id1=negative}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʌn.ɪmˈplɔɪ.mənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-unemployment.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of having no job<!--pertinent?, which may cause late [[filial life]]-->; joblessness.
#: {{ux|en|'''Unemployment''' made Jack depressed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=May and June|author=R. H. W. Bruce|title=The L.M.S.R. in Northern Ireland—I|journal=Railway Magazine|page=147|text=In 1928, an arrangement was made between the Government of Northern Ireland and the N.C.C. for the construction of a loop line as an '''unemployment''' scheme.}}
# The [[phenomenon]] of [[joblessness]] in an [[economy]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Unemployment''' has been considered a cause of crime.}}
# The level of joblessness in an economy, often measured as a percentage of the [[workforce]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Unemployment''' was reported at 5.2% in May, up from 4.9% in April.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A type of joblessness due to a particular economic mechanism.
#: {{ux|en|All '''unemployments''', seasonal, frictional, cyclical, classical, whatever, mean that you're out of work.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An instance or period of joblessness.
#: {{ux|en|Until then his life had consisted of low-paying jobs, numerous '''unemployments''', and drug use.}}

